{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Syntax and Variables in PHP"}, {"type": "position", "content": 8}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear in PHP."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand information about common solutions to problems in spoken or written documents.   Talk about programming and data base related to hypertext preprocessor in spoken or written documents. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace.  Understands specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts. "}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob08re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Reported Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>We use <strong>reported speech</strong> to say what another person said or asked.</p> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>We use&nbsp;<strong>reporting verbs</strong>&nbsp;to say what another person said. If we are<strong>&nbsp;reporting a question</strong>, we don&rsquo;t need to use the structure of a question, but the structure of an affirmative sentence.</p>  <p>In a direct question, we use the verb before the subject. In a <strong>reported question</strong>, we change the order back to an affirmative sentence, so we use the verb after the subject.</p>  <p>Example:</p> <strong>Direct question</strong>:&nbsp;<i>what is PHP?</i></br> <strong>Reported question</strong>:<i>&nbsp;she&nbsp;<strong>asked</strong>&nbsp;me what PHP&nbsp;<strong>was</strong>.</i></p> <p>As you can notice, we don't need any question mark. However, if we have to report a yes/no question, you need to add if.</p>  <p>Example:</p> <strong>Direct question</strong>:&nbsp;<i>Is this PHP?</i></br> <strong>Reported question</strong>:&nbsp;<i>he&nbsp;<strong>asked</strong>&nbsp;me if this&nbsp;<strong>was</strong>&nbsp;PHP.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, yesterday my brother asked me if I knew what PHP was, and I realized that I forgot some concepts about it."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Last class, Susie asked me if I knew what syntax and variables in PHP were."}, {"type": "example", "content": " Once in the middle of a class, the teacher asked us if we saw the similarity between PHP and magic tricks; no one understood until he said that, in both cases, you only show to others what you want. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Passive of Present Continuous"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the <span class=\"strong\"> passive </span> of <span class=\"strong\"> present continuous </span> to talk about actions that are happening in the moment of speaking, but the emphasis of the sentence is on the object."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div>To use the <strong>passive voice</strong> in <strong>present continuous</strong>, it is not necessary to mention the subject or doer of the action, when the doer of the action is not important or when we don't know who the doer of the action is.</div> <div>&nbsp;</div> <div>Follow this structure to write sentences in<strong> passive voice</strong> in the <strong>present continuous</strong> tense:</div> <div>&nbsp;</div> <div> <div style=\"text-align: center;\">Object + is/are + being + main verb in past participle</div> <div style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</div> </div> <div>Example:<i>&nbsp;A </i>website  is<i> <strong>being designed</strong><strong>.</strong></i></div> <div>&nbsp;</div> <div>If you want to mention the subject or doer you can use this structure:</div> <div> <div>&nbsp;</div> <div style=\"text-align: center;\">Object + is/are + being + main verb in past participle + by + subject.</div> </div> <div>&nbsp;</div> <div>Example:&nbsp;<i>A website <strong>is being designed by me.</strong></i></div>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "PHP is being executed on the server. "}, {"type": "example", "content": " As you can observe, a PHP script is being executed on the server, and the plain HTML result is being sent back to the browser. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "execute "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to carry out an order; to perform or do"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: PHP is being executed on the server."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob08aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bracket "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "pair of marks used to enclose words "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: All PHP codes start with brackets."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob08aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "question mark "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "marks used in questions "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  All PHP codes start with brackets and are also followed by a question mark."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob08aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "command "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a directive; an instruction or order to a computer program"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In other computer programming languages, such as Java, there is a command to declare a variable."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe1n1le01ob01aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"conten