s": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 12}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<b>Amy</b>: Hi, Christian. What are you doing?</br>\n<b>Christian</b>: Hi! Well, yesterday my brother asked me if I knew what PHP was, and I realized that I forgot some concepts about it. For this reason, I'm practicing and watching this video online. </br>\n<b>Amy</b>: Great! So, we can watch it together, and you can explain to me what happens in PHP; I still have trouble understanding what syntax is, can you show me? </br>\n<b>Christian</b>: Okay. Take a look at this example of PHP syntax: PHP is being executed on the server. As you can see, all PHP codes start with brackets and are also followed by a question mark. They finish with a question mark and a bracket. The order is not interchangeable, and the file extension is .php."}, {"time": "43500", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<b>Amy</b>: Last class, Susie asked me if I knew what syntax and variables in PHP were. Do you know what they are?</br>\n<b>Christian</b>: Good question. As you can observe, a PHP script is being executed on the server, and the plain HTML result is being sent back to the browser. As far as variables go, you can think of them as containers to store information. They have to be followed by the name of the variable. In other computer programming languages, such as Java, there is a command to declare a variable. On the other hand, in PHP you create a variable when you assign a value to it. Every time you assign a value to a variable, you should put it in quotation marks. Global and Local are examples of some variables in PHP. </br>\n<b>Amy</b>: And, what is a comment?"}, {"time": "86000", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<b>Christian</b>: Commenting is like a note for yourself or for other programmers. It doesn't show up on the webpage. Once in the middle of a class, the teacher asked us if we saw the similarity between PHP and magic tricks; no one understood until he said that, in both cases, you only show to others what you want. Anyways, always try to comment a lot so you don't forget what you're doing with your code. </br>\n<b>Amy</b>: Finally, how can I identify some PHP variables? </br>\n<b>Christian</b>: Well, variables are like containers for information; they must start with the dollar sign and then a letter or underscore mark. So, if you see those elements in a code, that's definitely a variable."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n8le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n8le01ob08re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}